<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Løgting</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Faroese Parliament</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Løgting is the parliament of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faroe-islands">Faroe Islands</a>. It descends from the Alting, the assembly of free men that met at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tinganes">Tinganes</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torshavn">Tórshavn</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viking-age">Viking Age</a> and is counted among the oldest parliamentary bodies in the world. Under Norwegian and then Danish rule it became mainly a law court and was abolished in 1816, but it was restored in 1852 and gained legislative powers under the Home Rule Act of 1948.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/logting.jpg" alt="Løgting" width="320" height="375" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marmelad · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_arms_of_the_Faroe_Islands.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
